{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552341-generally-accepted-house-rule-on-ritual-durations","id":1552341,"title":"Generally accepted House Rule on Ritual Durations?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-07-13T17:17:50-07:00","id":1552341,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Generally accepted House Rule on Ritual Durations?","content":"Edit: Thus is about the duration it takes to perform a ritual.<br /> <br /> So for rituals we have a the 3 basic categories in m20 based on successes: 1 to 5, 6 to 10, and 10+ successes. There are proposed optional rules on how long each roll is in M20: About 15 minutes for the 1st (max 2 rolls), 1 hour for the 2nd max 5 rolls, and 5 hours for the 3rd, max Arete+ Willpower rolls.<br /> <br /> What are people using in their chronicles? What makes sense in play?<br /> <br /> My thought is rather than work with the successes required as the benchmark, work with the number of rolls. And have each roll take successively longer.<br /> <br /> I.e You can make 1 roll for a rote or fast cast spell in combat. That 2nd roll? 15 minutes. Rolls 3 through 5? 1 hour each. Rolls 6 or higher are at 5 hours a pop.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2783,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","user":"Tytalus","date":". 07-13-2025, 11:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-14T01:11:22-07:00","id":1552359,"title":"","content":"Remember that within the fiction of the game, the time is defined by the complexity of the ritual. It so happens that more powerful effects require more complex rituals. The complexity of a ritual doesn't increase as you keep going, it's a result of your planning for it. That's why the normal rules call for the Mage to determine the target number of successes beforehand, and suggest the time for each ritual roll based on that.<br /> <br /> It's also the easiest way to keep effects under some control, as you define how much the character will have to prepare for the ritual based on the result they want to get.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, this is about putting logistical constraints in the actions of the characters. The more time required, the more the character have to prepare in advance to pull off the ritual without much problems. It's about what kind of effects you as ST want to keep from being used on the fly within a scene, or even only attempted as serious projects, because while it gives you direct risks, the number of successes alone doesn't quite capture this distinction.<br /> <br /> That said, anything may make sense depending on context. Those rules are guidelines, and while the players should expect them as the default you shouldn't feel constrained by them. Paradigm, effect and the impact of the spell in the story should be the main factor on determining how long it takes for any particular Magic, especially rotes. The optional rule makes it easy for when you want to roll a number of times and work with whatever comes up as successes, which is a good thing sometimes, and sometimes isn't. You can also tweak those times and ignore the rules when you think it would be better for a given rote. You can take into account how much preparation will go <i>before</i> the ritual to decide if the actual ritual really needs so much time, or could be simpler.<br /> <br /> So I personally mix and match those rules and a lot of improvisation. The normal rule is the best rule of thumb IMO, but I end up tweaking it more often than not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-14T05:19:38-07:00","id":1552364,"title":"","content":"Remember that there are rules for extended rolls that are not rituals. Picking up where you left off allows the mage to continue a failed or incomplete spell at +1 diff in the next turn. And that would take practically no time (just the regular time of the mage instruments).<br /> <br /> Rituals take longer and do not normally increase difficulty (unless botches,etc). <br /> <br /> Having said that, I normally follow M20 suggestion unless they do not fit well into the mage paradigm, meaning it could take longer or shorter time to do depending on them.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-14T22:41:54-07:00","id":1552402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552341-generally-accepted-house-rule-on-ritual-durations?p=1552359#post1552359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Remember that within the fiction of the game, the time is defined by the complexity of the ritual. It so happens that more powerful effects require more complex rituals. The complexity of a ritual doesn't increase as you keep going, it's a result of your planning for it. That's why the normal rules call for the Mage to determine the target number of successes beforehand, and suggest the time for each ritual roll based on that.<br /> <br /> It's also the easiest way to keep effects under some control, as you define how much the character will have to prepare for the ritual based on the result they want to get.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, this is about putting logistical constraints in the actions of the characters. The more time required, the more the character have to prepare in advance to pull off the ritual without much problems. It's about what kind of effects you as ST want to keep from being used on the fly within a scene, or even only attempted as serious projects, because while it gives you direct risks, the number of successes alone doesn't quite capture this distinction.<br /> <br /> That said, anything may make sense depending on context. Those rules are guidelines, and while the players should expect them as the default you shouldn't feel constrained by them. Paradigm, effect and the impact of the spell in the story should be the main factor on determining how long it takes for any particular Magic, especially rotes. The optional rule makes it easy for when you want to roll a number of times and work with whatever comes up as successes, which is a good thing sometimes, and sometimes isn't. You can also tweak those times and ignore the rules when you think it would be better for a given rote. You can take into account how much preparation will go <i>before</i> the ritual to decide if the actual ritual really needs so much time, or could be simpler.<br /> <br /> So I personally mix and match those rules and a lot of improvisation. The normal rule is the best rule of thumb IMO, but I end up tweaking it more often than not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gotcha. I just get annoyed that a 5 hour roll as a suggestion is a SINGLE roll. From what I understand, this is not how the fiction in the game works. Many magi would collapse after making a 5 hour, let alone 10 hour ritual. I see they can start again within 48 hours, but damn, that's very slow and I'm not a fan. It's why I'm curious what other people do in their games. Unless you can add High Ritual to your stamina for the duration you can cast and to your rolls... Maybe.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3153_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3153\" data-vbnamecard=\"3153\">Lord Revan</a>, I see what you're saying about getting another roll if you fail, which is an important note.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2783,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","user":"Tytalus","date":". 07-15-2025, 01:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T09:22:12-07:00","id":1552416,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tytalus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552341-generally-accepted-house-rule-on-ritual-durations?p=1552402#post1552402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I just get annoyed that a 5 hour roll as a suggestion is a SINGLE roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>But that roll is for a BIG project.<br /> <br /> The idea is that making such Magic requires extensive preparation, and is possibly going to cover days of rituals.<br /> <br /> And precisely because this is a big project, the characters are going to do a lot of preparation. They're going to prepare and protect a suitable location, to procure means to extend their stamina so they can go through the whole ritual, they're going to amass as many difficulty reductions as possible and so on, the whole nine yards.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying 5 hours is the best solution, but the reason for being so stringent is precisely to convey to the players how big such a project is and make them take the steps the characters would for such a thing. A Mage without the forethought and planning to deal with such a lengthy ritual shouldn't be thinking of committing to such a big work of Magic. It was never about doing so just with the traits you have in your sheet.<br /> <br /> Personally I'm not aggravated by the 5 hours time per roll, but I would lean heavily on the idea that planning and preparation should be able to reduce this time. The more effort the characters put into making this ritual special, including other lengthy Extended Actions, the more I would let this time be reduced.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T20:00:49-07:00","id":1552436,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tytalus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552341-generally-accepted-house-rule-on-ritual-durations?p=1552402#post1552402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3153_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3153\" data-vbnamecard=\"3153\">Lord Revan</a>, I see what you're saying about getting another roll if you fail, which is an important note.</div> </div> </div> </div>Remember that you can do that even if you didn't fail, but got an incomplete spell. For example, you have Arete 3 and you need 5 successes to do the effect. You get the whole 3 successes, which is a partial success. You can keep rolling at the next turn with +1 diff to try and get the 5 successes needed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T22:02:53-07:00","id":1552441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552341-generally-accepted-house-rule-on-ritual-durations?p=1552416#post1552416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But that roll is for a BIG project.<br /> <br /> The idea is that making such Magic requires extensive preparation, and is possibly going to cover days of rituals.<br /> <br /> And precisely because this is a big project, the characters are going to do a lot of preparation. They're going to prepare and protect a suitable location, to procure means to extend their stamina so they can go through the whole ritual, they're going to amass as many difficulty reductions as possible and so on, the whole nine yards.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying 5 hours is the best solution, but the reason for being so stringent is precisely to convey to the players how big such a project is and make them take the steps the characters would for such a thing. A Mage without the forethought and planning to deal with such a lengthy ritual shouldn't be thinking of committing to such a big work of Magic. It was never about doing so just with the traits you have in your sheet.<br /> <br /> Personally I'm not aggravated by the 5 hours time per roll, but I would lean heavily on the idea that planning and preparation should be able to reduce this time. The more effort the characters put into making this ritual special, including other lengthy Extended Actions, the more I would let this time be reduced.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a fair enough rule as a GM - that PC thought and effort can reduce the length of time per roll. I just don't like the &quot;Optional&quot; rule as suggested. However, one thing I have often pointed out to players is &quot;Do you want your enemies to be able to use this same rule against <b>you</b>?&quot; If it's 1 hour per roll, for any ritual... Well, those big powerful effects literally start becoming a lot easier for their enemies to do against them.<br /> <br /> And yes, if rituals are done 5/hours per day once per day, it means the big bad enemy's ritual is much easier to disrupt or harder to pull off. (But so are theirs)<br /> <br /> Grumble-grumble-mumble-something-jumble. I just wish get received better rules. :/","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}